  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B034",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B020",
    "B003"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged: B034 remains the strongest final choice. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock architecture is a distinct physical advance that enables atoms to serve as exceptionally precise and controlled references. Katori's originating concept and Ye's defining experimental development provide a compact, balanced attribution. Crucially, the achievement has progressed from an elegant proposal to independently reproduced measurement capability of extraordinary precision. That mature chain from physical idea to robust experiment is especially persuasive.",
    "case_against": "The historical lineage of optical lattice clocks is richer than two individuals, so the exact contribution of each proposed laureate requires final historical scrutiny. Future consequences, including redefinition of the second and some fundamental-physics applications, are still developing. The citation should therefore recognize the established clock invention and development, not anticipated institutional or technological outcomes.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength concept, defining experimental development, independent replication, and present measurement capability make B034 a complete and clearly attributable achievement.",
        "response": "I agree. His comparison with quantum error correction identifies the central distinction for me: B034 already joins principle, realization, and reproducible quantitative performance. This does not diminish the conceptual depth of B003, but it makes B034 the more mature prize configuration now."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson considers quantum error correction a fundamental resolution of decoherence, experimentally tested across platforms, while warning that transformative-scale fault-tolerant computation has not yet been realized.",
        "response": "I accept that the foundational physics of B003 is substantial and should not be judged merely by future commercial utility. Still, the unresolved boundary between demonstrated correction and unambiguous fault-tolerant performance at scale remains material when comparing candidates. It is why I retain B003 as an alternative rather than move it ahead of the fully established lattice-clock achievement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce finds B034 an unusually clean experimental achievement with clear attribution and exceptional reproducibility, but gives equal standing to high-energy neutrino astronomy because it opened a new observational channel.",
        "response": "I agree with his assessment of B034. For B008, the sustained signal makes the scientific opening important, but the incomplete source-specific astronomy and especially the unresolved question of whether one person can adequately represent the collaborative discovery keep it below B034 for me."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether Katori and Ye fully capture the originating conceptual and defining experimental lineage of optical lattice clocks.",
      "How narrowly the final citation should distinguish the demonstrated clock architecture from later metrological and fundamental-physics applications."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict apparent from the supplied profile. My long-standing attosecond-physics collaboration with Anne L'Huillier does not involve any proposed laureate or shortlisted achievement in this configuration."
  }
}
